.TH TROFF 6
.SH NAME
troff \- troff's macro packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B troff -mbook
.br
.B troff -man
.br
.B troff -ms
.br
.B troff -me
.br
.B troff -mm
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IR Troff (1)
reads a macro package before its input when given
.BI -m x\fR,
from
.BI /lib/tmac/tmac. x\fR.
.SS -mbook
A book: each macro one of LaTeX's, so that a book is readily turned
into LaTeX
.RI ( book2tex (1)).
.TP
.B ".title \fIT\fP  .subtitle \fIS\fP  .author \fIA\fP  .date \fID\fP  .maketitle"
The title page.
.TP
.B ".frontmatter  .mainmatter  .backmatter"
The book's parts: the front matter's folios roman, its chapters
unnumbered.
.TP
.BI ".part " title
.TP
.BI ".chapter " "title " \fR[ label \fR[ short \fR]]
.TP
.BI ".chapter* " title
.TP
.BI ".section " "title " \fR[ label \fR]
.TP
.BI ".subsection " "title " \fR[ label \fR]
.TP
.BI ".subsubsection " "title " \fR[ label \fR]
Headings; chapters open on right-hand pages (one-sided, with the
register
.B book-recto
0, on the next), a numbered chapter's sections numbered in it (1.1,
1.1.1), as the contents give them; a subsubsection neither numbered nor
in the contents.
A chapter's
.I short
title is its running head; a title too long for one beside the page's
number is said, and the chapter's number the head instead.
Long entries in the contents are filled short of their page's number,
hung after their numbers.
.TP
.B ".p"
A paragraph (or a blank line).
.TP
.B ".quote ... .endquote"
.TP
.BI ".epigraph ... .attrib " source " .endepigraph"
.TP
.BR ".fn ... .fe" ,  " \e*[fnmark]"
A footnote, its mark after the text a
.B \ec
ends (or where
.B \e*[fnmark]
is).
.TP
.BR ".en ... .ee" ,  " \e*[enmark]" ,  " .notes"
Endnotes, set under their chapters' names by
.BR .notes .
.TP
.BR ".label " \fIname\fP,  " \e*[ref " \fIname\fP],  " \e*[pageref " \fIname\fP]
A cross-reference: the chapter, section, figure or table, and the page.
.TP
.BI ".url " "address " \fR[\fPafter\fR]\fP
.TP
.BI ".link " "address text " \fR[\fPafter\fR]\fP
A URL, the address itself or
.I text
a link to it: never hyphenated, a line broken if need be after its
.B /
.B .
.B ?
.B &
.B =
.B #
.B _
.BR - ,
a link on each line it is set on; then
.I after
(its punctuation) joined to it.
.TP
.B .appendix
The chapters after it (a book's) are its appendices, Appendix A, B ...,
their sections A.1; an article's sections after it A, B, their
subsections A.1, as after LaTeX's
.BR \eappendix .
.TP
.BR \e*[TeX] ", " \e*[LaTeX]
TeX's and LaTeX's names as their logos are set (TeX's E lowered,
LaTeX's A small and raised), in the text or a heading, in its size and
font; read as the words in a PDF (searched, copied, heard), and written
as
.B \eTeX{}
and
.B \eLaTeX{}
by book2tex.
.B -ms
has them too.
.TP
.B ".contents"
The table of contents.
.TP
.BI ".cite " "key place" \fR[ ";" " key place" \fR]
.TP
.B ".bibliography"
Citations and the works cited, by
.IR refer (1).
.TP
.BI ".index " entry
.TP
.B ".printindex"
The index, its entries makeindex's
.RB ( sub!subentry ,
.BR sort@shown ,
.BR |see{...} ,
.BR |( ...
.BR |) ).
.TP
.BI ".figure " \fR[\fPlabel\fR]\fP " ... .caption " "text " .endfigure
.TP
.BI ".table " \fR[\fPlabel\fR]\fP " ... .caption " "text " .endtable
A figure or a table, numbered in its chapter (1.2), its caption below
or above what it captions as it is written; set where it is if
nothing waits and it fits, between paragraphs (written in a
paragraph, after it), else at the next
page's top; any waiting when a chapter ends set in it.
.TP
.B ".listoffigures  .listoftables"
Their lists.
.TP
.BR ".list " [ "1 a i bullet dash" "]  .item " [ mark "]  .endlist"
A list: numbered, lettered, in roman numerals, or marked, its marks
hung before its items' text; lists nest, each further in.
.TP
.BI ".image " "file \fR[\fPwidth \fR[\fPheight\fR]]"
A JPEG or PNG image, or a PDF's first page (a figure in vectors),
centred, kept on one page
.RI ( tr2pdf (1)'s
.BR "x X image" ).
What is not given (or given as
.BR \(dq\(dq )
is the image's own: the other by its proportions; with neither, its size
at its resolution, 72 to the inch if it says none (a PDF's, its page's);
and no wider than the line.
.TP
.BI ".alt " text
The next picture's or image's description, for those who cannot see
it.
.TP
.BI ".papersize " "w h \fR| a4 | a5 | b5 | letter"
.TP
.BI ".margins " "top bottom inner outer"
The page (6 by 9 inches; a size no page could be is refused, and said);
the registers
.B book-ps
and
.B book-vs
the type (11 on 14 points),
.B book-nps
and
.B book-nvs
the notes' (two points less), and
.B book-tvs
the contents' spacing (the text's): a thesis set double has its notes
and contents single; the string
.B book-lang
its language
.RB ( en ).
.PP
A paragraph's lines are broken all at once, as TeX breaks them
.RB ( ".ad pb" ),
its spaces able to shrink by a third
.RB ( .ssh ),
hyphens costing as loose lines do
.RB ( .hycost ),
one space after a sentence.
Notes are set at the foot of their marks' pages, in order, a long one
split between pages, a note within a note set below it,
each one's number full size, hung in the margin
(as Bringhurst sets them; raised at its head, with the register
.B book-fnhang
0); with the register
.B book-plain
1 there are no running heads, each page's number centred at its foot,
and with
.B book-chlatex
1 a chapter is headed as LaTeX's book class heads one (Chapter N above
its title, both bold, at the left); with
.B book-oneside
1 the binding margin is on the left of every page and every page is
headed by its chapter, and
.B ".ds book-adj pl"
sets the text ragged right; widows and orphans are kept from pages' edges, and
a page left short by them, or by a heading kept with its text, ends
where a full page does, its space spread between paragraphs and about
headings and displays
.RB ( "x X vstretch" ,
.BR vfoot );
the
PDF's contents, references, notes' marks and index are links, its
headings bookmarks.
The PDF is tagged
.RB ( "x X tag" ,
for
.IR tr2pdf (1)):
the book's parts, read in order, are its structure; its running heads
and folios are artifacts.
.SS -marticle
A journal's article: -mbook's macros, set with no chapters (sections
numbered 1, 1.1), its text in two columns, its title, authors and
abstract across the page at its head, its references a section at its
end. Letter paper, 10 points on 12, three-quarter-inch margins.
.TP
.B ".title \fIT\fP  .subtitle \fIS\fP  .date \fID\fP"
.TP
.BI ".author " name "  .affil " affiliation "  .email " address
Each author, set side by side, three to a row.
.TP
.B ".abstract \fR...\fP .endabstract  .keywords \fIK\fP"
Before
.BR .maketitle ,
in the title's block, indented; after, in the column.
.TP
.B .maketitle
The title's block; the first section makes it if it has not been made.
.TP
.BI ".columns " n
One column or two (the default), before the first text; one column's
margins are wider.
.TP
.BI ".figure* " label "  \fR...\fP  .endfigure*  .table* \fR...\fP .endtable*"
A figure or table across both columns, at the top of a page after the
one it is written on.
.TP
.BI ".section* " title
An unnumbered section (acknowledgements).
.PP
Each column ends flush at its foot (tr2pdf stretching what may
stretch); notes are set at the foot of the column their marks are in;
the last page's columns are balanced, its height measured on one pass
of
.IR book (1)
and used on the next.
Cite with
.B "refer -S numeric"
.RB ( "book -m article -S numeric" ).
.SS -mmemo
A technical memorandum, headed as Bell Laboratories' were, or a report:
-marticle's macros in one column.
.TP
.BI ".org " name
The organization, at the head's right.
.TP
.BI ".author " "name " \fR[ location \fR[ extension \fR]]
.TP
.BI ".case " N "  .file " F "  .number " TM
The case and file charged, the memorandum's number.
.TP
.BI ".memotype " kind
.RB ( "MEMORANDUM FOR FILE" ).
.TP
.BI ".kind " "memo\fR|\fPreport"
A memorandum's head (subject at the left; date, authors and number at
the right), or a report's (the title centred).
.TP
.B .signature
The authors' names at the end, at the right.
.SS -mslides
A talk's slides: -mbook's macros, a page a slide, 10 by 5.625 inches
(16:9), 20 points on 25 in Libertinus Sans, the text ragged and not
hyphenated, each slide's number at its foot.
What -mbook sets in a page is set in a slide: lists, equations,
tables, pictures, images, chess boards, links; figures and tables are
set where they are written, not floated.
An image
.RB ( .image )
given no size is its own, no wider than the line and no taller than
the room below it on the slide.
.TP
.B ".title \fIT\fP  .subtitle \fIS\fP  .author \fIA\fP  .affil \fIplace\fP  .date \fID\fP"
.TP
.B .maketitle
The title slide; the first slide or section makes it if it has not
been made.
.TP
.BI ".section " title " \fR[\fIlabel\fR]"
A slide of the title alone, beginning a part of the talk: a heading
(H2) in the tagged PDF and a bookmark, the slides after it below it.
.TP
.BR .slide " [\fItitle\fP [\fIlabel\fP]]"
A slide; what follows, to the next, is on it.
Its title (a heading and a bookmark) at the top; with none, its text
begins there.
.B \e*[ref
.IB label ]
is its number, a link to it.
.TP
.BR .item " [\fImark\fP]"
An item of a list; outside one, it begins a list of bullets, ended
with the slide.
-mbook's
.B .list
and
.B .endlist
make the others.
.TP
.B .pause
What follows is shown on the next page: the slide is set again with it
added, and what was shown already does not move (beamer's
.BR \epause ).
Each page before a slide's last is an artifact in the tagged PDF, with
no links on it: its structure and links are on its last page, read
once.
Its pages are labelled with the slide's number.
.TP
.B ".columns  .column \fR[\fPwidth\fR]\fP  .endcolumns"
Side by side: each
.B .column
begins one, half the measure wide unless
.I width
is given, the next beside it 0.4 inches on; the text after
.B .endcolumns
below the longest.
A display is centred in its column.
.B .pause
within columns is refused.
.TP
.B ".code \fR...\fP .endcode"
A program's text, as typed, in Libertinus Mono on a tint; a line
beginning with a dot is written
.BR \e&. .
.TP
.B ".aspect 16:9\fR|\fP4:3"
The page's shape, before the first slide (4:3 is 10 by 7.5 inches).
.TP
.B ".typeface sans\fR|\fPserif"
Libertinus Sans (the default) or Serif.
.TP
.BI ".accent " colour
The titles' colour
.RB ( #1d3f6e ).
.PP
A slide is diverted whole and set when the next begins, so that its
height is known: a slide too tall for its page is said on the standard
error, with by how much, and its text runs off the page rather than
onto another, as beamer's does.
A slide has no foot for notes:
.B .fn
is refused, and citations are numbered
.RB ( "refer -S numeric" ,
.BR "book -m slides" 's
default); refer's
.B .bibliography
is a slide of its own.
.SS -man
A manual page, as Plan 9's are written and set:
.BR .TH ,
.BR .SH ,
.BR .SS ,
.BR .PP ,
.BR .TP ,
.BR .IP ,
.BR .HP ,
.BR .RS ,
.BR .RE ,
.BR .TF ,
.BR .EX ,
.BR .EE ,
.BR .PD ,
and the fonts'
.B .B
.B .I
.B .L
and their alternations
.RB ( .BR ,
.BR .IR ...);
.BI .MR " page section" \fR[\fPpunctuation\fR]\fP,
groff's reference to a page,
.IR page ( section ).
.IR Man (1)
.B -t
sets a page so.
.SS -ms -me -mm
Plan 9's, as Neatroff reads them: papers, Berkeley's, and the
memorandum macros.
.SH FILES
.B /lib/tmac
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR book (1),
.IR man (1),
.IR troff (1)
.br
.B docs/TROFF.md
in the source tree.
